**Read:** [Engineering Statics — Baker and Haynes](../sources/Engineering-Statics-Baker-and-Haynes.lingua#r_3DXGKGQSPPA9YDNH2WSB8XRN80), sections 1.1–1.4. Follow the shared units Record next and return with a declared basis for each quantity.

Length, time, mass, force, angle, torque, power and temperature describe different observations. A number such as 500 means little without the quantity, unit, location and operating condition. Mass is not force: in a declared local gravitational field, weight has magnitude mg. A rated maximum is not necessarily the present operating value.

For an original illustration, a 12 kg object has weight approximately 117.72 N if g = 9.81 m/s² is assumed. A support carrying that static load may see a different force during acceleration or impact. Keep the assumed model visible rather than attaching the same number to every use of the object.

**Work:** Make a small table of measured or stated quantities for your selected machine. Explain how each could be observed and distinguish a nominal size, measurement, tolerance and operating limit. Use the shared mathematics only as far as this question requires, then return to the machine.

